{"id":"n-20261011-23ny3d","category":"tech","display_title":"The Rising Calendar Phishing Scam","display_title_he":"הונאת הפיishing ביועץ הלוח הולה","short_summary":"Cybersecurity experts warn about an exponential surge in calendar phishing scams, where fraudulent meeting requests and notifications are automatically added to digital calendars to steal user credentials.","english_level":"C1","words":458,"reading_time":3,"image":null,"sources":[{"name":"The Guardian","domain":"theguardian.com","url":"https://www.theguardian.com/money/2026/oct/11/calendar-phishing-scam-meeting-renewal-google-microsoft-paypal","title":"‘You have a meeting’: the calendar phishing scam growing exponentially","published_at":"2026-10-11T06:00:18.000Z"}],"published_at":"2026-10-11T06:00:18.000Z","first_published_in_app_at":"2026-10-11T13:11:04.767Z","quality_score":9,"interest_score":9,"verification_status":"verified","content_status":"published","paragraphs":["A deceptive cybersecurity threat known as calendar phishing is experiencing exponential growth across digital platforms, according to specialists in the industry. As professionals prepare their weekly schedules, they increasingly encounter unexpected calendar entries that appear to be forgotten meetings or critical service renewals. When users interact with these uninvited notifications, they often find descriptions containing links or phone numbers designed to siphon sensitive data. The resulting data theft allows fraudsters to sell credentials in batches, breach corporate email accounts, or impersonate trusted institutions like banks.","According to Luke Wescott, a threat detection engineer at Sublime Security, the mechanism relies on the default settings of popular electronic scheduling tools. Calendar applications such as Google Calendar frequently add invitations automatically without requiring the user to explicitly accept them. Consequently, malicious actors do not even need their targets to open an incoming email or route it out of the spam folder. These unauthorized entries gain borrowed credibility by appearing alongside legitimate personal commitments, such as dentist appointments or weekly one-on-one sessions with a supervisor.","Furthermore, researchers note that malicious actors sometimes leverage legitimate platforms like Zoom to transmit these invitations, making them exceptionally difficult for security software and artificial intelligence blockers to filter out without inadvertently blocking valid communications. Max Gannon, an intelligence analysis manager at Cofense, explains that these notifications can be customized to closely resemble internal company communications, complete with organizational logos. The content varies widely, encompassing fabricated notifications such as new voicemail receipts, unusual activity warnings for PayPal, impending auto-payment processing notices, or invitations to bid on contracts.","The content typically prompts recipients to visit a counterfeit login page modeled after services like Microsoft, Google, or PayPal to enter their passwords, or to dial a support number regarding a fabricated financial charge that never actually occurred. Experts emphasize that individuals are not compromised until they actively click the fraudulent links or place the calls. The attackers do not possess total access to the victim's calendar beforehand; rather, they merely use this innovative delivery method to execute standard phishing techniques.","To mitigate these risks, specialists recommend cultivating a healthy level of skepticism regarding all unexpected calendar prompts, regardless of how authentic they may appear. Max Gannon advises individuals to adopt a paranoid mindset, treating every invitation with suspicion even if it appears to originate from someone nearby in the workplace. Users are specifically advised to adjust their calendar application settings to disable automatic additions, ensuring that invitations are only accepted from known senders or after manual acceptance. Additionally, security professionals caution against clicking the decline button on suspicious calendar items, as this action merely confirms to the sender that the target email address is active; instead, recipients should simply delete the item or report it as spam or junk."],"facts":[{"fact":"Calendar phishing is growing exponentially, using automated calendar invitations to lure victims into credential theft.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Scammers exploit the automatic invitation features of apps like Google Calendar, bypassing the need for users to open emails.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Cybersecurity experts from Sublime Security and Cofense warn that entries often mimic legitimate software renewals, meetings, or voicemails.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"To protect against this threat, users are advised to turn off automatic meeting acceptances and treat unexpected calendar notifications with suspicion.","sources":[1]}],"glossary":[{"text":"A","lemma":"a","pos":"DET","he":"מ","name":"False"},{"text":"deceptive","lemma":"deceptive","pos":"ADJ","he":"מטעה","name":false},{"text":"cybersecurity","lemma":"cybersecurity","pos":"NOUN","he":"אבטחת מידע","name":false},{"text":"threat","lemma":"threat","pos":"NOUN","he":"איום","name":false},{"text":"known","lemma":"know","pos":"VERB","he":"ידוע","name":false},{"text":"as","lemma":"as","pos":"ADP","he":"כ","name":false},{"text":"calendar","lemma":"calendar","pos":"NOUN","he":"לוח שנה","name":false},{"text":"phishing","lemma":"phishing","pos":"NOUN","he":"פישינג","name":false},{"text":"is","lemma":"be","pos":"AUX","he":"הוא","name":false},{"text":"experiencing","lemma":"experience","pos":"VERB","he":"חווה","name":false},{"text":"exponential","lemma":"exponential","pos":"ADJ","he":"אקספוננציאלי","name":false},{"text":"growth","lemma":"growth","pos":"NOUN","he":"צמיחה","name":false},{"text":"across","lemma":"across","pos":"ADP","he":"ברחבי","name":false},{"text":"digital","lemma":"digital","pos":"ADJ","he":"דיג